        "subtitle": "Reading the language of the face",
        "text": "We rely on facial signals all the time, yet we cannot define them. We read a language we cannot put into words and may not even consciously notice. Good face reading can give an insight into a person's true feelings, and although real skill takes practice, anyone can learn the basics.\n\nWe have twenty-two facial muscles on each side of the face, more than any other animal. Like most muscles, they are anchored in bone, but unlike most, they are attached to the skin, which makes the face more mobile than the skin on other parts of the body. A few of these muscles are especially important. The zygomatic major is the smile maker: it runs across the cheek to the corner of the mouth, which it pulls upwards. The corrugator draws the eyebrows together, making vertical lines between them. Most pleasant expressions use the zygomatic major, and most unpleasant ones the corrugator. The busiest muscle is the frontalis, the curtain-like muscle of the forehead, which raises the eyebrows in fear or for emphasis. Its opposite, the procerus, lowers the eyebrows in sadness. When the mouth is pulled back horizontally in fear, we use the risorius.\n\nThese signals appear in infancy, on a reliable timetable. The smile and surprise are there at birth; disgust and distress (sadness) appear between birth and three months; the \"social smile\" at one and a half to three months; anger at three to seven months; and fear at five to nine months. Anger is a dark look of concentration: the eyebrows go down and the lips tighten. An angry face is a warning. Fear has almost the opposite features: the eyes widen and the eyebrows rise. Surprise looks like fear and often comes just before it: the eyes and mouth open wide and the eyebrows rise and arch. Disgust centres on the nose, which is otherwise not a very expressive part of the face. It may turn up slightly, wrinkle and tighten, as when we notice a bad smell.\n\nWhat about telling lies? On the whole, we are not very good at spotting them. Even people we would expect to be skilled, such as judges, police officers and psychiatrists, do only slightly better than average. Prisoners spot lies fairly well, perhaps because they live in a world of deceit and have had to become expert. In the eighteenth century, Gottfried Leibniz suggested that studying the face and voice could improve lie detection, and he was right.\n\nThere is no single sure sign of a lie, so good lie detectors look for several. One clue is smiling. Liars show fewer genuine smiles and more masking smiles, and these, together with a higher voice, are the best evidence of deception. True smiles make small crinkles in the skin near the eyes; false smiles usually do not. False smiles may also come too early or too late, stop suddenly instead of fading, or be slightly uneven. Honest expressions do not last long: any expression longer than ten seconds, and most that last longer than five, are probably false. Surprise is especially brief. It always lasts less than a second, and it starts and stops suddenly; if it goes on longer, the person is faking it.\n\nAccording to popular belief, rapid blinking is a sign of lying. This is true of some lies, but blinking is a general response to strong feelings such as excitement or anxiety. And looking away? We do tend to look down and away out of guilt or shame, but eye movements are an unreliable clue: liars know that some people rely on them, and use the fact. Strangely, pushing someone for the truth can actually hide a lie from us: under pressure, some liars make more eye contact and use more facial expression, which convinces their questioners that they are honest."
